From 6c7dcad872701e4cb160fa559ae692c8a4e98e6e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Burke Cates <burke.cates@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 08:30:43 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] NOT-FOR-UPSTREAM: soundwire: qcom: don't let a no-ALERT status read hide ALERT
 -> ATTACHED

The CHANGE_ENUM_SLAVE_STATUS handler skips a SWRM_MCP_SLV_STATUS value
equal to ctrl->slave_status, which stands for the status last handed to
the SoundWire core. qcom_swrm_get_alert_slave_dev_num() also updates
that cache (through qcom_swrm_get_device_status()), but when it finds
no peripheral in ALERT the SLAVE_PEND_IRQ handler does not pass the
status on. A later status change to the same value is then skipped.

That is what happens after a peripheral alert: the core services it
(the peripheral goes ALERT -> ATTACHED), the re-latched SLAVE_PEND_IRQ
finds nobody in ALERT any more and caches the new ATTACHED status, and
the CHANGE_ENUM edge for the same transition reports "Slave status not
changed". The core keeps the peripherals in ALERT until the status
changes again. With clock stop across system sleep it doesn't: on the
ASUS Zenbook A16 (two WSA8845 per bus) a parity error on one s2idle
resume left both amplifiers of a bus in "Alert" for the next 68
resumes. The amplifiers themselves were fine (status register 0x14,
both ATTACHED, and audio played), but a peripheral the core holds in
ALERT counts as attached, so a later fault on it would not be told
apart.

Keep the cached value when the status read in the SLAVE_PEND_IRQ
handler was not handed over, so the CHANGE_ENUM handler reports the
ALERT -> ATTACHED change.

Assisted-by: Claude Code (claude-opus-5-5)
---
Not-for-upstream: local workaround for one laptop, not reviewed for
  submission; use only for local testing.

 drivers/soundwire/qcom.c | 4 +++-
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/soundwire/qcom.c b/drivers/soundwire/qcom.c
index 095d361e4e1d..b2e20221a3e9 100644
--- a/drivers/soundwire/qcom.c
+++ b/drivers/soundwire/qcom.c
@@ -984,7 +984,7 @@ static void qcom_swrm_recover_work(struct work_struct *work)
 static irqreturn_t qcom_swrm_irq_handler(int irq, void *dev_id)
 {
 	struct qcom_swrm_ctrl *ctrl = dev_id;
-	u32 value, intr_sts, intr_sts_masked, slave_status;
+	u32 value, intr_sts, intr_sts_masked, slave_status, reported;
 	u32 i;
 	int devnum, loops = 0;
 	int ret = IRQ_HANDLED;
@@ -1037,10 +1037,12 @@ static irqreturn_t qcom_swrm_irq_handler(int irq, void *dev_id)
 
 			switch (value) {
 			case SWRM_INTERRUPT_STATUS_SLAVE_PEND_IRQ:
+				reported = ctrl->slave_status;
 				devnum = qcom_swrm_get_alert_slave_dev_num(ctrl);
 				if (devnum < 0) {
 					dev_err_ratelimited(ctrl->dev,
 					    "no slave alert found.spurious interrupt\n");
+					ctrl->slave_status = reported;
 				} else {
 					sdw_handle_slave_status(&ctrl->bus, ctrl->status);
 					wake_up_all(&ctrl->slaves_wait);
-- 
